+// LLIntEntryPtrTag shares OperationPtrTag's category exactly -- native code
+// entered from JIT-generated code -- and separates a different thing: the
+// ENTRY CONTRACT of the callee. An operation is a C function and obeys the
+// platform ABI. An LLInt or IPInt entry point is offlineasm output and obeys
+// JSC's own conventions. On ARM64E and x86-64 those two contracts are the
+// same thing, which is why one tag served both. On PPC64LE they are not:
+// ELFv2 says a C function derives its TOC from r12 at its global entry and
+// finds its caller's frame through the back chain at 0(r1), while an LLInt
+// entry point has no global entry, never reads r12, and is entered with
+// r1 == cfr, where 0(r1) is the live JS frame's CallerFrame rather than spare
+// linkage area. MacroAssemblerPPC64::jumpTargetIsNativeCode() is the
+// consumer; the note above emitIndirectJump there has the measurements.
+//
+// The category is shared deliberately. Every ARM64E path this tag takes --
+// the JITOperationList assertion, the JIT-cage operation map, the signing
+// implementation, and the JumpSignatureType farJump picks -- is then the one
+// OperationPtrTag already took, so the only thing that differs on that
+// architecture is the diversifier constant, whose signing and authenticating
+// sites are the same two lines of the same function.
 #define FOR_EACH_JSC_PTRTAG(v) \
     /* Callee:Native Caller:None */ \
     v(DisassemblyPtrTag, PtrTagCalleeType::Native, PtrTagCallerType::None) \
@@ -68,6 +87,8 @@ using PtrTag = WTF::PtrTag;
